# Accessibility Standards (WCAG AA)

Use this rule when building or reviewing UI to ensure WCAG 2.1/2.2 AA compliance.

---

## 1. Color Contrast

**WCAG AA minimum contrast ratios:**

- **Normal text (< 18pt or < 14pt bold):** 4.5:1
- **Large text (≥ 18pt or ≥ 14pt bold):** 3:1
- **UI components and graphical objects:** 3:1

**Do:**
- Test contrast with browser DevTools or online tools (WebAIM Contrast Checker)
- Use dark text on light backgrounds or light text on dark backgrounds
- Ensure links have sufficient contrast against their background (not just hover state)

**Don't:**
- Use low-contrast gray text for critical content or primary actions
- Rely on color alone to convey meaning (pair with icon, text, or pattern)

---

## 2. Keyboard Navigation

**Do:**
- Ensure all interactive elements are keyboard-accessible (links, buttons, form controls, custom widgets)
- Support Tab (forward), Shift+Tab (backward), Enter/Space (activate), Esc (close modals/dialogs)
- Maintain logical tab order (follows visual/reading order)
- Provide keyboard shortcuts for primary actions when helpful (document them)

**Don't:**
- Create keyboard traps (focus must be able to move away from any element)
- Hide or disable focus indicators (see Focus Visible below)
- Require mouse-only interactions for critical tasks

---

## 3. Focus Visible

**Do:**
- Use visible focus indicators (outline, ring, border, background change) with at least 3:1 contrast
- Use `:focus-visible` to show focus for keyboard but not mouse clicks (if framework supports)
- Ensure focus states are consistent (same style for all interactive elements)

**Don't:**
- Use `outline: none` without a replacement focus indicator
- Make focus indicators too subtle (1px dotted outline is often insufficient)

**Example:**
```css
/* Good: visible ring for keyboard, no ring for mouse */
.focus-ring {
  @apply focus:outline-none focus-visible:ring-2 focus-visible:ring-blue-500;
}
```

---

## 4. Semantic HTML

**Do:**
- Use semantic elements: `<nav>`, `<main>`, `<header>`, `<footer>`, `<section>`, `<article>`, `<aside>`
- Use heading hierarchy (`<h1>` → `<h2>` → `<h3>`) without skipping levels
- Use `<button>` for actions, `<a>` for navigation
- Use native form controls (`<input>`, `<select>`, `<textarea>`) when possible

**Don't:**
- Use `<div>` or `<span>` for interactive elements (use `<button>` or `<a>`)
- Skip heading levels (e.g. `<h1>` → `<h3>`)
- Use generic containers when semantic elements fit

---

## 5. ARIA (Use Sparingly)

**Do:**
- Use ARIA only when semantic HTML isn't sufficient
- Common valid uses: `aria-label`, `aria-labelledby`, `aria-describedby`, `aria-expanded`, `aria-current`, `role="dialog"`
- Test with screen readers (NVDA, JAWS, VoiceOver)

**Don't:**
- Use ARIA to override native semantics when not needed (e.g. `<button role="button">` is redundant)
- Add ARIA without testing with assistive tech
- Use `aria-hidden` on focusable elements

**Example:**
```tsx
// Good: button with dynamic state
<button aria-expanded={isOpen} aria-label="Open menu">☰</button>

// Good: section with heading
<section aria-labelledby="about-heading">
  <h2 id="about-heading">About</h2>
</section>
```

---

## 6. Forms

**Do:**
- Associate labels with inputs using `<label>` and `for` / `htmlFor`
- Provide clear error messages near the field (not just color)
- Show required fields explicitly (not just `*`)
- Use `autocomplete` attributes for common fields (name, email, address)

**Don't:**
- Use placeholder-only labels (placeholders disappear on focus)
- Rely on color alone to indicate errors (pair with icon or text)
- Hide labels visually without providing screen-reader-only text

**Example:**
```tsx
// Good: visible label + clear error
<label htmlFor="email">Email address (required)</label>
<input id="email" type="email" aria-describedby="email-error" />
{error && <p id="email-error" className="text-red-600">{error}</p>}
```

---

## 7. Images & Media

**Do:**
- Provide meaningful `alt` text for informative images
- Use `alt=""` for decorative images (announces nothing to screen readers)
- Provide captions or transcripts for video/audio
- Ensure text in images is also available as real text

**Don't:**
- Leave `alt` attribute missing (fails validation and screen readers)
- Use "image of" or "picture of" (redundant; screen readers announce "image")
- Embed critical text in images without a text alternative

---

## 8. Interactive Elements

**Do:**
- Minimum touch/click target: 44×44px (CSS pixels) for primary actions
- Provide clear hover, focus, and active states
- Ensure buttons and links have descriptive labels (not "click here")
- Use `aria-label` or `aria-labelledby` for icon-only buttons

**Don't:**
- Create tiny click targets (< 24px) for critical actions
- Use generic labels ("Submit", "Click here") without context
- Rely on hover-only affordances (ensure tap/keyboard works)

---

## 9. Page Structure

**Do:**
- One `<main>` landmark per page
- Skip links ("Skip to main content") for keyboard users when there's heavy nav
- Unique, descriptive page titles (`<title>`)
- Use landmarks (`<nav>`, `<main>`, `<aside>`) for screen reader navigation

**Don't:**
- Have multiple `<main>` elements on the same page
- Use ambiguous page titles ("Home", "Page")

---

## 10. Motion & Animation

**Do:**
- Respect `prefers-reduced-motion: reduce` (disable or reduce animation)
- Keep essential motion under 5 seconds; provide pause/stop for longer
- Use animation to support understanding, not distract

**Don't:**
- Auto-play video/audio without controls
- Flash content more than 3 times per second (seizure risk)
- Use motion as the only indicator of state change

---

## Quick WCAG AA Checklist

- [ ] Color contrast: 4.5:1 text, 3:1 UI
- [ ] Keyboard: all interactive elements accessible, no traps
- [ ] Focus: visible indicators, consistent, 3:1 contrast
- [ ] Semantic HTML: headings, landmarks, native controls
- [ ] ARIA: only when needed, tested with screen readers
- [ ] Forms: labels, error messages, required indicators
- [ ] Images: meaningful alt text or alt=""
- [ ] Touch targets: min 44×44px for primary actions
- [ ] Page structure: one main, skip links if needed, unique titles
- [ ] Motion: respect prefers-reduced-motion, no flashing

---

## Testing Tools

- **Browser DevTools:** Lighthouse (Accessibility audit), Inspect element accessibility tree
- **Contrast:** WebAIM Contrast Checker, browser DevTools color picker
- **Screen readers:** NVDA (Windows), JAWS (Windows), VoiceOver (Mac/iOS)
- **Keyboard:** Tab through the page; ensure all actions work without mouse
- **Automated:** axe DevTools, WAVE browser extension

